      "content": "i get that, but isn't that a bit of a stretch? wouldn't you say the communal/religious stuff is actually a stabilizing force for someone? like, having a strong community and family support usually means better mental health and less chance of sudden health crises. \n\nit feels like you're penalizing him just for being social and having people around. if we look at the data more closely, is there any specific reason why large gatherings or religious lifestyles are inherently more 'volatile' than someone just living a solitary life?",
